  "text": "The Celts took a river as a sacred force and more often as a goddess. A river bore the name of the lady who ruled its bed or who broke loose to set the water running. This lady fed the people beside her with fish, water for pulses, and water for cattle. She stood as one of the mother kind the Deae Matres. The water in her stream ranked with the milk of the cow in the Celtic mind. The rise of a river held a great healing in the folk view. The folk dropped gifts at the source for a cure from a sickness or a limb. A warm spring ranked the higher in cure. At Bath in England, the Celts and Romans came to the hot Baths for the pain of a joint. A dip in the warm water was held to still a deep arthritis. Holy wells and rivers also carry tales of the Fairies and the dead. Some set this link as a trace of the old faith. A river in Celtic belief shows the gods as male and the river as female alike.",
